<!--
  Sentry DSN for the native alarm core (project: reminder-android).

  Committed on purpose. A DSN is NOT a secret: it is write-only, public by
  design, and ships inside the APK regardless — anyone can unpack the app and
  read it. Keeping it in the repo means a fresh clone builds a working,
  reportable app instead of a silently unreported one, which is the whole point
  of having crash reporting.

  Crash.kt looks this up by name and does nothing if it is absent, and
  AndroidManifest.xml sets io.sentry.auto-init=false so the SDK never
  initialises itself behind our back (it crashed the app on launch when it did —
  see that file's comment).
-->
